1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
The moment your tools run in a loop, you hit a problem.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A tool fails, and if you're not careful, the whole loop crashes.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Some calls are also risky enough that you don't want the model firing them unsupervised.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This lesson covers handling errors, gating high-impact calls,

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and how agentic dispatch drives the loop.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Let's set up three ideas that run through this whole lesson.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
First, agentic dispatch.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
That's the pattern where the model,

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
not your code, chooses which tool to call each turn,

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
and your harness executes that choice and feeds the result back.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, the execution loop.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
That's the cycle you run around CLOD, inspect the stop reason,

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
run the returned tool use, append a tool result, and re-invoke until CLOD is done.

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Third, the approval gate.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
That's a checkpoint that pauses a high-impact call for confirmation before it runs,

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
and it lives in your runtime, not in the model's judgment.

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Agentic dispatch for who chooses.

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
The execution loop for how you drive it,

19
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
and the approval gate for what you hold back.

20
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Those three frame everything ahead.

21
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Let's start with agentic dispatch because it's the mindset shift.

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
In an agentic loop, the model steers and your harness executes.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
CLOD looks at the current state of the task and selects the tool that fits this particular step.

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Your code runs that tool and captures whatever comes back, the observation.

25
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Then that observation goes right back into the next request, and CLOD decides what to do next.

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Notice what's not happening here.

27
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
You're not writing a big if-then tree that says,

28
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
if the user asked for weather, call the weather tool.

29
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
The model makes that call from the tool descriptions and the context.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Your job shifts from deciding what to run to reliably running what the model chose and reporting the result.

31
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
It helps to contrast this with the older way of doing things.

32
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
In a hard-coded branch, your code decides which function to call using fixed if-then logic.

33
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
You inspect the input, match it against conditions you wrote, and dispatch to a function.

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
That's predictable, but it's brittle, and it's blind to nuance the model could pick up from the request.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Agentic Dispatch flips that.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
CLOD decides which tool fits each turn, reading the user's request and your tool descriptions.

37
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
And your loop simply executes the choice and feeds the result back.

38
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
The trade-off is real.

39
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
You give up some determinism, but you gain the model's ability to handle requests you never

40
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
explicitly coded a branch for.

41
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
For open-ended tasks, that flexibility is usually the whole point.

42
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Now to the failure that syncs naive loops.

43
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
A tool throws an error, and the developer lets that exception bubble up and kill the whole loop. Don't do that.

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
When a tool fails, hand the failure back to CLOD instead.

45
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
You put the error text into the tool result content, and USET is error to true.

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
That tells CLOD the call failed, and importantly, it lets CLOD see why.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
From there, the model can retry with different arguments, fall back to another approach, or tell the

48
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,000
user what went wrong.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
A returned error keeps the loop alive and recoverable.

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
A raised exception just ends it.

51
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Treat a tool failure as information to pass along, not a reason to crash.

52
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Let's separate three kinds of failure because they're handled differently.

53
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
First, a tool execution error.

54
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The tool ran and genuinely failed, a timeout, a bad state, a missing record.

55
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
You return the reason in a tool result with is error true, and you make the message instructive.

56
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Something like rate limit exceeded, retry after 60 seconds.

57
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Not just the word failed.

58
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Second, a malformed parameter call.

59
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
CLOD sent arguments that don't work.

60
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
You return an error, and CLOD retries with corrections, usually within a couple of attempts.

61
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
A better long-term fix is a sharper description or strict tool use.

62
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Third, a server tool error.

63
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
That one fails on Anthropic's side, and CLOD handles it transparently.

64
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
So you don't return an is error for it at all.

65
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Let's linger on that third case because it's a common mistake.

66
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
When a server tool fails, that error doesn't flow through your error handling.

67
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
CLOD catches it transparently on its own side.

68
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
So you don't set is error, and you don't assemble a tool result for it the same way

69
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
you never assembled a successful one.

70
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
A web search, for example, might hit a rate limit or get invalid input or exceed its allowed

71
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
uses, and CLOD deals with those internally.

72
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The rule is clean.

73
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Only client tool failures come back to your code for you to handle.

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Server tool failures are Anthropic's to manage, and trying to handle them yourself just adds

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
code that never runs.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Before we leave error handling, a few message format rules will bite you if you don't know them.

77
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
And they usually show up as a 400 error.

78
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
First, a tool result has to immediately follow the tool use it answers.

79
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
You can't slip another message in between.

80
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Second, when you send tool result blocks in a user turn, they have to come first in that

81
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
message, before any text you add.

82
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Put text ahead of the tool result, and the request is rejected.

83
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Third, there's no separate tool or function role in this API.

84
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Results ride inside normal user messages, and the tool use blocks ride inside assistant messages.

85
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Get that ordering right, put the results first, and keep them adjacent to their calls.

86
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And the loop just runs.

87
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Let's talk about the calls you don't want firing unsupervised.

88
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Some actions are high impact, deleting data, sending money, emailing a customer.

89
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And for those, you want an approval gate.

90
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,000
You have a few ways to build one.

91
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
You can gate in the runtime, pausing the call in your loop, and requiring a confirmation

92
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
before it executes, not after.

93
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
And if you're running inside the agent SDK harness, you can use a pre-tool use hook,

94
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
which inspects a call and blocks, or allows it, before the tool runs.

95
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
The one thing you shouldn't do is leave the decision to the model.

96
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Don't rely on Claude to self-police a dangerous action.

97
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Enforce the gate in code you control, so a risky call always stops for a human or a rule

98
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
before it goes through.

99
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Here's the bottom line.

100
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
A resilient loop returns tool failures as tool result with isError true, so Claude can

101
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
recover instead of crashing.

102
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:01,000
It matters because the model drives dispatch, and it can only adapt to a failure that it can see.

103
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Just remember, server tool errors are Claude's to handle, and high impact calls belong behind

104
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
a gate that you control.

105
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
That covers errors, approval gates, and agentic dispatch.

106
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
I'll see you in the next one.

